<div class="mt-10" id="topic-rules-of-inference">

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold">
        8. Rules of Inference
    </h2>

    <div class="w-full h-[1px] flex items-center justify-center pt-[10px]">
        <div class="bg-gray-600 w-full h-[1px]"></div>
    </div>

    <p class="text-md text-gray-400 px-3 mt-3 leading-relaxed">Rules of inference are valid argument patterns used to derive a conclusion from one or more premises. If the premises are true and a valid rule is applied correctly, the conclusion must be true.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto mt-4"><table class="w-full border-collapse"><thead><tr class="border-b border-[#3A2560] text-white"><th class="p-3">Rule</th><th class="p-3">Pattern</th><th class="p-3">Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class="border-b border-[#3A2560]"><td class="p-3 text-white">Modus Ponens</td><td class="p-3">\(p,\; p → q \;	herefore q\)</td><td class="p-3">From p and p implies q, conclude q.</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-[#3A2560]"><td class="p-3 text-white">Modus Tollens</td><td class="p-3">\(¬ q,\; p → q \;	herefore ¬ p\)</td><td class="p-3">If q is false, p cannot be true when p implies q.</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-[#3A2560]"><td class="p-3 text-white">Hypothetical Syllogism</td><td class="p-3">\(p → q,\; q → r \;	herefore p → r\)</td><td class="p-3">Chains two implications.</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-[#3A2560]"><td class="p-3 text-white">Disjunctive Syllogism</td><td class="p-3">\(p \lor q,\; ¬ p \;	herefore q\)</td><td class="p-3">If p or q is true and p is false, q must be true.</td></tr><tr class="p-3 border-b border-[#3A2560]"><td class="p-3 text-white">Addition</td><td class="p-3">\(p \;	herefore p \lor q\)</td><td class="p-3">A true proposition can be combined with another using OR.</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-[#3A2560]"><td class="p-3 text-white">Simplification</td><td class="p-3">\(p \land q \;	herefore p\)</td><td class="p-3">From a true conjunction, either conjunct may be concluded.</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-[#3A2560]"><td class="p-3 text-white">Conjunction</td><td class="p-3">\(p,\; q \;	herefore p \land q\)</td><td class="p-3">If both propositions are true, their conjunction is true.</td></tr><tr><td class="p-3 text-white">Resolution</td><td class="p-3">\(p \lor q,\; ¬ p \lor r \;	herefore q \lor r\)</td><td class="p-3">Combines disjunctions to eliminate complementary terms.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h3 class="text-xl font-bold mt-6">Example: Modus Ponens</h3><div class="bg-[#1B0D2D] border border-[#3A2560] rounded-lg p-5 space-y-2"><p class="text-md text-gray-400 px-3 mt-3 leading-relaxed"><span class="text-white font-semibold">Premise 1:</span> If I submit the assignment, then it is recorded. \((p → q)\)</p><p class="text-md text-gray-400 px-3 mt-3 leading-relaxed"><span class="text-white font-semibold">Premise 2:</span> I submit the assignment. \((p)\)</p><p class="text-md text-gray-400 px-3 mt-3 leading-relaxed"><span class="text-white font-semibold">Conclusion:</span> The assignment is recorded. \((q)\)</p></div><p class="text-md text-gray-400 px-3 mt-3 leading-relaxed">A valid rule of inference preserves validity, but the truth of the premises must still be established when evaluating an argument in practice.</p>
</div>
